export abstract anchor FactoryEvolution:
    reviewSchedule:
        A project-configured scheduler in code creates a periodic review order.
        The trigger is the later of a configured interval, defaulting to one
        week, and a configured number of completed orders. Missed runs catch up
        once, duplicate triggers debounce, and an idle project reviews only when
        enough new evidence exists. The engineer may submit a review order
        directly. A mid-tier agent executes the review as a normal order; it is
        not a core command and not the recurring scheduler.
    proposalRecord:
        An enhancement proposal records its identifier, observed evidence,
        expected benefit, estimated cost and risk, affected requirements or
        operations, reuse case, priority, authority and budget impact, state,
        and links to its source evidence. States are proposed, approved,
        scheduled, completed, rejected, and superseded. The record is evidence
        and is linked from project-owned Piton.
    evidenceInputs:
        The review uses completed, failed, blocked, cancelled, and escalated
        outcomes; recurring blockers and repeated manual steps; review and
        correction cost; routing results with model and category; and resource
        data. Recurring means at least a configured number of occurrences in a
        configured window with a stable deduplication key. Missing or delayed
        usage is reported as uncertain rather than treated as zero.
    approvalBoundary:
        Proposals follow the enhancement approval policy. Engineer approval is
        required unless a prior authorization explicitly covers the proposal's
        scope, duration, and budget. Specification-change authority and
        implementation-change authority are separate grants. An approved
        enhancement cannot change the approval policy, budget enforcement,
        validation gate, audit, or rollback mechanism under the same grant.
    enhancementLifecycle:
        Approved work is scheduled as a normal order or step with its own
        acceptance criteria and an independent assessor. The enhancing order
        completes under the pre-change implementation. Activation is a separate
        step after that order terminates. The implementation records a version
        for the changed component and exercises rollback before activation; if
        no tested rollback exists, the change is not applied automatically.
        A failed validation, failed acceptance, regression against existing
        conformance, or failed rollback enters a safe blocked state and
        escalates.
    routingEvidence:
        Cold start uses an engineer-approved default model order for each
        category. A routing change needs a rolling evidence window, defaulting
        to ninety days, with at least twenty accepted outcomes across at least
        two models and at least five accepted outcomes per model. A configured
        exploration share, defaulting to ten percent, routes work to a
        non-preferred approved model so counterfactual evidence accumulates.
        Outcome cost includes review passes and corrections.
    categoryLifecycle:
        Work outside the starter categories is recorded as unclassified and can
        accumulate provisional evidence. Adding, merging, splitting, or retiring
        a category requires engineer approval and the evidence threshold.
        Categories with no activity for the rolling window are candidates for
        retirement but are never removed silently.
    modelLifecycle:
        A new model is approved by the engineer before use, with a recorded
        benchmark or canary plan. Provider model changes and deprecations
        trigger re-approval or removal. Routing records the approved-model set
        and the evidence-configuration version it used, not the billing
        conversion table.
    evolutionConformance:
        Separately verdicted cells cover: scheduled review creation and debounce;
        insufficient-evidence skip; proposal state transitions; unauthorized
        scope rejection; pre-authorized acceptance; independent assessment;
        rollback success, failure, and safe block; cold start; exploration
        quota; threshold with per-model minimums; category addition, merge, and
        retirement; and model re-approval. Each cell records evidence and an
        observed outcome.
